course: physics
lesson: 26
title: Static electricity: balloons, hair and sparks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Everything is made of tiny bits that carry electric charge, and rubbing two things together can move some of the tiniest, called electrons, from one onto the other.
explain: Rub a balloon on your hair and electrons move from your hair to the balloon; opposite charges pull, so your hair reaches for the balloon, while hairs with the same charge push each other apart and stand on end.
explain: When enough charge builds up it can jump across a gap as a spark, like the tiny zap from a door handle after you shuffle across the carpet, or the crackle of a jumper pulled off in the dark.
explain: Lightning is the same thing on a gigantic scale: ice and water bumping inside a storm cloud build huge charges that leap to the ground as a giant spark.
explain: A lightning rod gives that spark an easy metal path down into the earth, so it does not go through the building.
fact: Rubbing a balloon on your hair moves electrons onto the balloon, so the balloon and your hair carry opposite charges and pull on each other.
fact: Things with the same charge push each other apart, so charged hairs stand on end.
fact: A charged balloon sticks to a wall because it pulls on the charges in the wall; on a damp day the charge leaks away and it falls sooner.
fact: A rubbed comb can pick up bits of paper and even bend a thin stream of tap water.
fact: Walking on carpet can build up charge on your body, which jumps to a metal door handle as a tiny spark, more often in winter when the air is dry.
fact: A jumper crackles and sparks in the dark when you take it off, because rubbing fabrics build up charge and each crackle is a tiny spark.
fact: Clothes stick together after the tumble dryer because tumbling charges them, while clothes dried on a line in open air hardly ever cling, and a dryer sheet tumbled with them stops the charge building up.
fact: Dust sticks to a TV screen because the screen can carry a little charge, and a damp cloth removes both the charge and the dust.
fact: Lightning often strikes the same tall place again and again; the Empire State Building in New York is hit around twenty times a year.
fact: Lightning forms when ice and water bumping in a storm cloud build up huge charges that jump to the ground as a giant spark.
fact: For a split second a lightning bolt is hotter than the surface of the Sun.
fact: A lightning rod gives lightning an easy metal path down a thick wire into the earth, and Benjamin Franklin invented it.
exercise: What happens when you rub a balloon on your hair? | Electrons move from your hair onto the balloon | The balloon makes new electricity from nothing | Your hair turns into tiny magnets | Air from the balloon sticks to your hair
exercise: Why does your hair stand up after you rub a balloon on it? | Each hair has the same charge, so they push apart | The balloon pulls every hair up with a kind of suction | Your hair gets hot and lighter | The hairs are scared of the balloon
exercise: Do opposite charges push apart or pull together? | They pull together | They push apart | They do nothing to each other | They swap places
exercise: Why does a rubbed balloon stick to the wall? | Its charge pulls on the charges in the wall | The rubbing makes the balloon's skin a bit sticky | The wall is magnetic | The air pressure pins it there
exercise: Why does the balloon fall off the wall sooner on a damp day? | The charge leaks away into the damp air | Damp walls are too slippery | Damp air is heavier and pushes the balloon down | The balloon gets wet and too heavy
exercise: What can a comb rubbed on your hair pick up? | Small bits of paper | Heavy coins from a purse | Big steel nails and bolts | A full glass of water
exercise: Why do you get a tiny shock touching a door handle after walking on carpet? | Charge built up on you jumps to the metal as a spark | The handle is plugged into the mains | The carpet is wired to the door | Your shoes are leaking electricity
exercise: When do door-handle shocks happen most often? | In winter, when the air is dry | In summer, when the air is damp | Only on rainy days | Only at night
exercise: What is the crackle when you pull off a jumper in the dark? | Tiny sparks from charge built up by rubbing | The wool fibres snapping one after another as you pull | Air escaping from the sleeves | Your hair breaking
exercise: Why do clothes stick together after the tumble dryer? | Tumbling charges them, and charged clothes pull on each other | The dryer melts them a little | They are still too wet | The dryer uses glue to soften them
exercise: Why does dust stick to a TV screen? | The screen can carry a charge that pulls dust | The screen is sticky to the touch | The light coming from it attracts the dust to it | Dust likes warm places
exercise: How can you clean dust off a screen so it stays off longer? | Wipe it with a damp cloth, which removes the charge | Rub it hard all over with a woolly jumper until it shines | Blow on it very hard | Shine a torch on it
exercise: Does lightning ever strike the same place twice? | Yes, tall places are hit again and again | Never, not even once in exactly the same place | Only once in every hundred years or so, at most | Only if the place has moved since the last time
exercise: About how many times a year is the Empire State Building hit by lightning? | Around twenty times | Never | Once every ten years | Around a thousand times
exercise: What builds up the charge in a storm cloud? | Ice and water bumping into each other | The Sun shining hard on top of the cloud | The wind blowing through the trees below | Birds flying through it and rubbing it
exercise: Who invented the lightning rod? | Benjamin Franklin | The scientist Isaac Newton | Inventor Thomas Edison | The astronomer Galileo
exercise: What does a lightning rod on a church spire do? | Gives lightning an easy metal path down into the earth | Attracts more lightning so it hits the church even harder | Stores the lightning for later use | Pushes storm clouds away
test: Nan says lightning never strikes the same place twice, so the spot where it hit is safe now. right? | No, lightning often hits the same tall place again and again | Yes, it can never strike there again | Yes, but only for a year | No, it strikes exactly twice and then stops
test: A thin stream of water from the tap bends towards a rubbed comb. What does this show? | The charged comb pulls on the water | The comb has turned into a small magnet | The water has partly frozen into ice | The comb blows a little air at the water
test: argh, every time I touch the car door I get zapped. why's it worse in winter? | Dry winter air lets charge build up on you | The car is plugged in during winter | Cold metal makes its own electricity in winter | Your coat is wired to the car
test: In a cartoon, lightning hits a character, who only gets sooty hair and a funny face. What is the truth? | Lightning is a giant spark that can kill, far hotter than the Sun's surface | Lightning only tickles | Lightning cannot hit people | Lightning only makes hair curly
test: What would happen if a tall church had no lightning rod and was struck? | The lightning could pass through the building and damage it | Nothing, churches cannot be struck | The lightning would bounce straight back up into the storm cloud | The church would store the charge for ever
test: Which is true about a crackling jumper and a bolt of lightning? | Both are sparks from built-up charge, one tiny and one huge | They have nothing at all in common | Lightning is made of wool fibres | A jumper's crackle is only a sound from the wool, with no spark at all
test: Your clothes dried on the washing line outside never cling. Why? | They were not rubbed and charged by tumbling | The Sun makes them magnetic | The wind glued them apart | Clothes only ever cling in summer, when it is hot
test: bet u can't stick this balloon on the ceiling without tape. How could you do it? | Rub it on your hair and hold it up to the ceiling | Fill it with water | Freeze it first | Paint it with glitter
test: Two balloons both rubbed on your hair hang side by side on strings. What do they do? | Push away from each other, because they have the same charge | Snap together tightly | Pop at once | Hang touching, doing nothing
test: Why is it a bad idea to stand on a hilltop or under a lone tall tree in a thunderstorm? | Lightning tends to strike tall, high things | Trees catch fire from rain | Hills attract hail only, and hail can be deadly | Wind is stronger under trees
test: Why do dryer sheets help stop static cling? | They stop charge building up on the clothes | They make the clothes heavier so they cannot cling | They make the dryer hotter | They glue the clothes apart
exam: Identify the principal process whereby a rubber balloon acquires an electric charge upon being rubbed against hair. | The transfer of electrons from the hair to the balloon | The creation of new charge within the rubber by the heat of friction | The magnetisation of the rubber | The absorption of air by the hair
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the behaviour of two bodies bearing like charges? | They repel one another | They attract one another | They exert no influence upon one another | They exchange their charges instantaneously
exam: It is asserted that lightning does not strike the same location on more than one occasion. Select the response that is consistent with the established facts. | Tall structures are struck repeatedly, some many times within a single year | The assertion is correct without exception | Lightning strikes each location exactly twice | Lightning never strikes structures of great height
exam: Ascertain the function of a lightning conductor affixed to a tall building. | To provide a path of low resistance whereby the discharge is conveyed safely to earth | To attract a greater number of discharges for storage | To repel thunderclouds from the vicinity | To amplify the discharge in order to protect adjacent buildings
